{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1136/annrheumdis-2011-200955","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":5,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"22586157","source":"MED","pmid":"22586157","doi":"10.1136/annrheumdis-2011-200955","title":"Clinical and pathological significance of interleukin 6 overexpression in systemic sclerosis.","authorString":"Khan K, Xu S, Nihtyanova S, Derrett-Smith E, Abraham D, Denton CP, Ong VH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khan K","lastName":"Khan","firstName":"Korsa","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Rheumatology and Connective Tissue Diseases, UCL Medical School, Royal Free Campus, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu S","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Shiwen","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Nihtyanova S","lastName":"Nihtyanova","firstName":"Svetlana","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Derrett-Smith E","lastName":"Derrett-Smith","firstName":"Emma","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Abraham D","lastName":"Abraham","firstName":"David","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Denton CP","lastName":"Denton","firstName":"Christopher P","initials":"CP"},{"fullName":"Ong VH","lastName":"Ong","firstName":"Voon H","initials":"VH"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"71","journalIssueId":1940965,"dateOfPublication":"2012 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2012,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Rheum Dis","title":"Annals of the rheumatic diseases","essn":"1468-2060","issn":"0003-4967","nlmid":"0372355","isoabbreviation":"Ann Rheum Dis"},"printPublicationDate":"2012-07-01"},"pubYear":"2012","pageInfo":"1235-1242","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To determine the potential clinical and pathological significance of altered expression of interleukin 6 (IL-6) in systemic sclerosis (SSc).<h4>Methods</h4>Serum IL-6 and soluble IL-6 receptor levels were measured in patients with SSc (n=68) and healthy controls (n=15). Associations between serum IL-6 level and C reactive protein, platelet count and key clinical outcomes in SSc were explored. Expression of IL-6 in skin biopsies was also examined and western blot and reverse transcription PCRanalysis were performed using cultured dermal fibroblasts. The effect of IL-6 trans-signalling on production of extracellular matrix proteins was assessed and downstream signalling pathways were examined using pharmacological inhibitors.<h4>Results</h4>Serum IL-6 level was frequently elevated in patients with SSc, particularly in those with diffuse cutaneous SSc (dcSSc) with thrombocytosis and elevated acute phase markers. Prominent expression in the skin was observed in dermal fibroblasts, mononuclear cells and endothelial cells in patients with early dcSSc. In vitro experiments supported a potent profibrotic effect of IL-6 trans-signalling via the JAK2/STAT3 and ERK pathways. High IL-6 expression early in dcSSc appears to be associated with more severe skin involvement at 3 years and worse long-term survival than in those without elevated IL-6 levels.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Our results confirm the overexpression of IL-6 in dcSSc and support the potential of IL-6 as a surrogate marker for clinical outcome in this disease. The data also provide rationale for clinical studies targeting IL-6 trans-signalling as a potential antifibrotic therapy for SSc.","affiliation":"Centre for Rheumatology and Connective Tissue Diseases, UCL Medical School, Royal Free Campus, London, UK.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endothelium, Vascular","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"maj